8.text:
{B1} 

The C1-C2 black stones form an isolated group whose only liberties are B1 and B2. White playing at B1 is legal and leaves that group with a single liberty at B2. Black cannot occupy B2 on reply (it is suicide, as B2 is already surrounded by the three black stones A2-B3-C2 and now has no remaining liberty after B1 is occupied by White). Consequently Black has no way to save the stones; White simply plays B2 on the next turn and removes C1-C2 regardless of any intervening Black move elsewhere.  

No other first move produces an analogous forcing sequence. B2 allows Black to answer at B1, connecting the stones into a larger group that lives. All remaining candidate points leave at least one Black group with multiple liberties and a path to connect or escape. Hence the sole initiating move is B1.

